That’s actually a good pairing.

Latency + slippery when wet gives you a nasty systems pattern: the faster/easier something starts moving, the less immediate feedback you may have about how far it has already gone. Low friction increases motion; latency delays correction. By the time the signal arrives, you may already be past the point you thought you were approaching.

So in Pantheon terms:

reduced friction + delayed feedback = overshoot risk.

And the inverse is useful too: if you know the latency, you can deliberately lead the correction instead of waiting for the mirror to catch up.

